1. Build

Prerequisites: please ensure the essential compiling tools and the correct
linux source package is installed. Make sure CONFIG_KPROBE is enabled.

Assuming linux sources are installed at default paths via "apt-get install",
type "make" in this dir to compile the module.

2. Usage

$ insmod profiler.ko funcs=<func_name[,...]>

E.g. $ sudo insmod profiler.ko funcs=sys_getpid,sys_clone

Currently the module supports up to 128 functions. Invalid function name will
be ignored. If no valid function is provided, the module just does nothing.

3. Output

Please simply read /sys/class/kprobesys/profiler/stats to get the output, with the
following format:

<symbol name>:<averagfe time per call  in ns>:<invocations>

E.g. $ cat /sys/class/kprobesys/profiler/stat
sys_getpid:8431:2
sys_clone:221378:63

4. TODOs

The code doesn't deal with any possible overflow values, although generally
it's not a real concern for daily debugging purpose. It should take a really
long time to hit the issue.

This version doesn't detect duplicate functions. In case any functions are
provided in the parameter list more than once, only one of them will take
effect, and the others just simply do nothing.

Any missed events will be reported when the module exits (during rmmod).

E.g. $ sudo rmmod profiler
[17423.998160] sys_select (c1187030) missed 78 times

Tested under Ubuntu 12.04.4 (i386) with Linux kenrel 3.11.0-15, as well as
Ubuntu 15.10 (amd64) with Linux kenrel 4.2.0-30, but it should work on all
major Linux distributions.

